      "body": "⚠️ severity: IMPORTANT —\n⚠️ **IMPORTANT: Nested composite GitLab keys pass the tracker layer but are rejected by the validation gate this same PR adds**\n\nThe tracker layer fully supports nested namespaces — `gitlabIssueNumberIn` parses `group/subgroup/project#12` (`js/common/ticketKeyShapes.js:53-59`, pinned by the test_trackers.js case \"composite nested-namespace keys carry their own project path\"), and `issueArgs` splits it into workspace/repository. But the gate extended here rejects exactly that shape (verified against this branch):\n\n```\nisValidTicketKey('group/subgroup/project#12')              === false\nvalidateTicketKeyFormat('group/subgroup/project#12')       throws \"Invalid or missing ticket key\"\nextractTicketKeyFromJql('key = group/subgroup/project#12') === null\n```\n\nRoot cause: the GitHub composite shape `[\\w.-]+\\/[\\w.-]+#\\d+` matches only one slash, `isGitLabRouterKey` only `gl-N`, and `shapeSources()` still derives the JQL extraction alternation from `GITHUB_KEY_SHAPES` only — so there is no GitLab composite branch in either the gate or the extractor. A workflow seeded with `inputJql: \"key = group/subgroup/project#12\"` aborts in the pre-action with `Invalid or missing ticket key: null`, and `buildEncodedConfig` (js/common/buildEncodedConfig.js:488) rejects the same key — while the header this PR writes documents \"composite namespace/project#N form (nested subgroups supported)\" (js/common/trackers.js:41-43).\n\nSuggested fix, keeping the single-owner doctrine of gh-770: add the is-shape twin (`GITLAB_KEY_SHAPES` including the nested composite), an `isGitLabKeyShape()`, include it in `isValidTicketKey` here, and derive the extraction alternation from both shape lists. The negative space is already pinned by `gitlabIssueNumberIn returns null for non-GitLab keys`, so the extended gate can be pinned the same way."
